    "content": "In a move to draw attention to their working conditions and demands for better pay, hundreds of ride-share drivers for Uber and Lyft in Nashville, Tennessee, have decided to begin a strike on Friday, August 30th, at Nashville International Airport. This comes as the city gears up to welcome a large influx of tourists for the Labor Day holiday weekend, with tourism spending reaching a record high of $30.6 billion in 2023.

The recently established Tennessee Drivers Union (TDU) is spearheading this initiative to raise awareness about their needs for improved compensation and working conditions. The strike will commence at the airport and will be followed by a procession of ride-share drivers through Nashville's Lower Broadway, a popular tourist destination known for its entertainment district.

According to the TDU, the drivers are facing significant challenges, including allegations that Uber and Lyft can take between 60 to 80 percent of each ride fare. They are also advocating for enhanced bathroom facilities at the Nashville airport, restrictions on the nighttime use of scooters, a limit on the number of ride-share drivers on the road, and a ban on out-of-state drivers to mitigate delays in securing fares.

"Most of the drivers in the Tennessee Drivers Union (TDU) work 12-hour days every day of the week doing rideshare. They depend on rideshare to be able to make a living, and Uber and Lyft provide their sole income," the TDU stated in a press release. "The drivers refuse to continue to let huge companies like Uber and Lyft profit off of its labor. They want Nashville to hear their struggle."

